  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a light-driven setup for running many chemical reactions in parallel and monitoring them electronically. On the left, an array of LED lights shines down onto a standard 96-well plate, suggesting that each well can be illuminated at once for high-throughput experiments. The emphasis is on controlled light exposure across many small reaction vessels rather than on a single reaction.\n\nOn the right, the image zooms in on a device beneath or associated with one well: a photodiode built on a silicon wafer with an electrode pattern on top. Incoming light hits the photodiode, which generates an electric current. That current is shown as directly driving a chemical reaction, implying a link between light intensity and electrochemical activity.\n\nTaken together, the figure conveys a platform that combines plate-based screening, illumination, and microscale electronics to trigger or study reactions in parallel. The main idea is that light is converted into electrical current by the silicon photodiode, and this current powers chemistry at the electrode surface. Some fine structural details are simplified, but the overall concept of scalable, light-addressed electrochemistry is clear.",
